<!-- ============ HOW RFID WORKS ============
     Straight from the approved script. The UPC-versus-EPC distinction is the
     thing every other explanation on this page depended on and never stated. -->
<section class="ac-blue sunk section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">The Foundation</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">RFID provides the signal. We turn it into action.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Hold an RFID tag up to the light. You can see it: a set of fine lines, that's the antenna, connected to a tiny chip. That chip stores a number, permanently. No battery required. It just needs a burst of energy from an RFID reader, a gun, a pad, a scanner, to wake up and transmit its ID.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="intx-grid intx-grid-3">
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">01</span>
        <h3>That ID is an Electronic Product Code</h3>
        <p>Unlike a barcode, which identifies an item that can have many units, an EPC identifies a specific unit of the item.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">02</span>
        <h3>One UPC, ten EPCs</h3>
        <p>Say you have ten identical shirts on a shelf: one UPC, but ten different EPCs. Scan them all at once, and that's your count of ten, instantly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">03</span>
        <h3>You've seen this at self-checkout</h3>
        <p>Place your units in a bin and the cart appears on screen automatically. Readers inside the bin blast the tags with energy, capture each EPC, and the system builds the transaction. Again, instantly.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

